220d344b5\"],[[\"The state has forfeited thousands of crores in central grants and challenged fund withholdings in the Supreme Court rather than capitulate to the three-language mandate.\",null,null,[[2138,2307],[2128,2297]]],[1],[[\"https://thesouthfirst.com/telangana/done-telangana-paradox-cm-says-new-education-policy-soon-but-gradual-rollout-of-nep-is-already-on/#:~:text\\u003dBut%20the%20state%20eventually%20gave%20in%20to,a%20decision%20by%20a%20four%2Dmember%20Cabinet%20subcommittee.\",\"Telangana paradox: CM says new education policy soon, but gradual rollout \\nof NEP is already on\",\"https://encrypted-tbn3.gstatic.com/favicon-tbn?q\\u003dtbn%3AANd9GcQxhHL-ltEShGH_962gFjoN3S-Jiqx8OsmvC9J_ydt1AsB25kM-nXuSEIYX8icwCWLFvdzkJbIGNMVLWD3_qmWp4d7A2BHscAmLTO4x\",\"But the state eventually gave in to the funding pressure and signed the MoU \\nto unlock roughly ₹1,150 crore in the withheld Samagra Shiksha funds. In \\nJune 2026, ...\",[[\"The state has forfeited thousands of crores in central grants and challenged fund withholdings in the Supreme Court rather than capitulate to the three-language mandate.\",null,null,[[2138,2307],[2128,2297]]]],null,\"The South First\",32,\"\"]],\"spp_a276306beb2443fb\"]]],null,null,null,null,null,[2],\"en\",null,null,[null,null,null,null,[[[\"http://googleusercontent.com/youtube_content/1\"],null,null,null,[[[\"Is the three language formula actually working? | Focus Tamil Nadu\",\"vPTY8pRAPkA\",\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v\\u003dvPTY8pRAPkA\",\"The Hindu\",null,[\"Chapter 4 of Part I of the Report of the High-Level Committee on Union-State Relations deals with perhaps Tamil Nadu’s most sensitive issue — language. But this is not Tamil versus Hindi.  This is about what the Constitution actually says, whether Hindi numbers are inflated, whether English needs protection and whether India’s language policy needs a reset. It also challenges three assumptions: that Hindi is spoken by most Indians; migration makes Hindi essential, and the Three-Language Formula is working.\\n\\nThank you for watching our video!\\n\\nYou can subscribe to our channel here: https://bit.ly/3c8Adi6 \\n\\nVisit https://www.thehindu.com/ for the latest updates, analysis, opinions, and more. \\n\\nYou can also download our apps:\\nAndroid: https://bit.ly/3nboBEi\\niOs: https://apple.co/3EDCwt4\\n\\nThe Hindu is committed to keeping you up-to-date with information on the developments in India and the world. We promise to deliver quality journalism that stays away from vested interest and political propaganda. You can support us by subscribing to our digital offerings here: https://bit.ly/3emywiz \\n\\nFollow us: \\nTwitter: https://twitter.com/the_hindu \\nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/the_hindu/\"],null,18709,null,\"PT6M49S\",\"2026-02-26\",[\"c_e87b6c6ac16404a5_f1db0bcb-365f-42f6-85bc-e2ff0f3d3f99\"],\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/vPTY8pRAPkA/maxresdefault.jpg\"]]]]],null,null,[]],null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[],null,[[\"c_e87b6c6ac16404a5_f1db0bcb-365f-42f6-85bc-e2ff0f3d3f99\",null,null,null,null,[null,null,null,null,[[[\"http://googleusercontent.com/youtube_content/1\"],null,null,null,[[[\"Is the three language formula actually working? | Focus Tamil Nadu\",\"vPTY8pRAPkA\",\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v\\u003dvPTY8pRAPkA\",\"The Hindu\",null,[\"Chapter 4 of Part I of the Report of the High-Level Committee on Union-State Relations deals with perhaps Tamil Nadu’s most sensitive issue — language. But this is not Tamil versus Hindi.  This is about what the Constitution actually says, whether Hindi numbers are inflated, whether English needs protection and whether India’s language policy needs a reset. It also challenges three assumptions: that Hindi is spoken by most Indians; migration makes Hindi essential, and the Three-Language Formula is working.\\n\\nThank you for watching our video!\\n\\nYou can subscribe to our channel here: https://bit.ly/3c8Adi6 \\n\\nVisit https://www.thehindu.com/ for the latest updates, analysis, opinions, and more. \\n\\nYou can also download our apps:\\nAndroid: https://bit.ly/3nboBEi\\niOs: https://apple.co/3EDCwt4\\n\\nThe Hindu is committed to keeping you up-to-date with information on the developments in India and the world. We promise to deliver quality journalism that stays away from vested interest and political propaganda. You can support us by subscribing to our digital offerings here: https://bit.ly/3emywiz \\n\\nFollow us: \\nTwitter: https://twitter.com/the_hindu \\nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/the_hindu/\"],null,18709,null,\"PT6M49S\",\"2026-02-26\",[\"c_e87b6c6ac16404a5_f1db0bcb-365f-42f6-85bc-e2ff0f3d3f99\"],\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/vPTY8pRAPkA/maxresdefault.jpg\"]]]]]],null,[[1789201906,35189000],null,1,false,false],[],\"youtube_74304ce3-cc04-4975-b0f1-0e121174faa2\",13,null,false]]]],[[\"NEP three language policy resistance southern states 2025 2026\",1],[\"Tamil Nadu NEP PM SHRI funds language row\",1]],null,\"rc_939b6b1f0f34805b\",[[null,[\"google\",[null,null,\"Google Search\",\"https://www.gstatic.com/images/branding/productlogos/googleg/v6/192px.svg\",null,\"Searching the web\"],1]],[null,[\"google\",[null,null,\"Google Search\",\"https://www.gstatic.com/images/branding/productlogos/googleg/v6/192px.svg\",null,\"Searching the web\"],4]],[null,[\"google\",[null,null,\"Google Search\",\"https://www.gstatic.com/images/branding/productlogos/googleg/v6/192px.svg\",null,\"Searching the web\"],1]],[null,[\"google\",[null,null,\"Google Search\",\"https://www.gstatic.com/images/branding/productlogos/googleg/v6/192px.svg\",null,\"Searching the web\"],4]]],[[null,[\"google\",[null,null,\"Google Search\",\"https://www.gstatic.com/images/branding/productlogos/googleg/v6/192px.svg\",null,\"Searching the web\"],4]]],null,null,\"IN\",true,null,null,[[[[null,[null,0,\"While it looks like a clean sweep on paper, what is actually happening is institutional coercion rather than true cultural or linguistic assimilation.\"]],[null,[null,0,\"The Centre has accumulated structural leverage over education, but attempting to homogenize a civilization as polycentric as India comes with steep costs and hard limits.\"]],[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[]],[null,[null,3,\"Where the Hegemonic Project Is Working\"]],[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[[[[[null,[null,0,\"Fiscal Arm-Twisting: The Centre tied critical welfare and infrastructure funding—like the Samagra Shiksha Abhiyan (SSA) and PM SHRI model schools—directly to states adopting the NEP. States struggling with revenue deficits (such as Kerala) were eventually pressured into signing MoUs just to unlock thousands of crores in withheld educational funds.\",[[0,20,[[null,null,null,null,2]]],[21,182,[null,null,null,[null,[\"spp_6dca05b42ed54bff\"]]]],[183,349,[null,null,null,[null,[\"spp_42d714b5a0ca4333\"]]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"Control of Central Boards: CBSE, ICSE, Navodaya Vidyalayas, and Kendriya Vidyalayas serve the mobile middle class, civil servants, and military families across every state. By enforcing NEP norms in these systems, the Union government bypasses state education ministries entirely, normalizing Hindi and Sanskrit among urban youth in non-Hindi capitals.\",[[0,26,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"National Competitive Exams: Key national entrance gates (NEET, CUET, SSC, UPSC, and central banking exams) remain structurally tilted toward English and Hindi. Even when regional language options are added, question phrasing, source material, and answer keys are overwhelmingly developed in Hindi or English, forcing ambitious students nationwide to build literacy in those two tongues.\",[[0,27,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"Asymmetric Hindi Spending: Budgets for the promotion of Hindi and Sanskrit dwarf allocations for classical languages like Tamil, Telugu, Kannada, or Malayalam by orders of magnitude, effectively subsidizing the infrastructure of northern linguistic spread.\",[[0,26,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]]]]],[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[]],[null,[null,3,\"Why the Project Hits a Wall\"]],[null,[null,0,\"Despite that institutional muscle, equating policy decrees with actual homogenization ignores several critical counter-currents:\"]],[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[[[[[null,[null,0,\"Tamil Nadu’s Iron Wall: Tamil Nadu remains the structural barrier to total uniformity. Across party lines—from DMK to AIADMK and new regional outfits—the state strictly enforces its 1968 two-language policy. The state has forfeited thousands of crores in central grants and challenged fund withholdings in the Supreme Court rather than capitulate to the three-language mandate.\",[[0,23,[[null,null,null,null,2]]],[87,207,[null,null,null,[null,[\"spp_8126be5220d344b5\"]]]],[208,377,[null,null,null,[null,[\"spp_a276306beb2443fb\"]]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"Classroom vs. Street Reality: Passing a third-language Hindi exam to meet board criteria in Karnataka, Telangana, or Maharashtra rarely translates to social adoption. Students memorize enough to clear the board requirement, then discard it in favor of their mother tongue locally and English globally. Formal compliance does not equal cultural assimilation.\",[[0,29,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"Linguistic Re-Assertion in Other Southern States: Pushback is no longer isolated to Tamil Nadu. In Karnataka, agitations by Kannada groups over bank signage, central exams, and metropolitan service desks have intensified. The language question has increasingly turned into a flashpoint over local jobs and demographic shifts caused by inter-state migration.\",[[0,49,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"The Looming Delimitation Shock: The deeper friction point isn't just school textbooks—it’s parliamentary representation. If parliamentary seats are redrawn based on raw population, the northern states (which lagged in family planning) stand to gain massive legislative power over the southern states (which successfully controlled population growth and generate disproportionate tax revenue). Language imposition acts as the cultural canary in the coal mine for this broader federal crisis.\",[[0,31,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]]]]],[null,[null,0,\"The RSS and the central apparatus can use administrative carrots and sticks to mandate languages on school report cards. However, turning a federation of historically rooted linguistic nationalities into a unified, monolingual cultural bloc faces deep organic resistance.\"]],[null,[null,0,\"A grounded analysis of how this policy plays out on the ground can be seen in the explainer Is the three language formula actually working?.\",[[92,139,[null,[\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v\\u003dvPTY8pRAPkA\"]]]]]],[null,[null,0,\"This report examines whether the Three-Language Formula is actually fulfilling its stated educational goals or merely deepening federal and regional fault lines.\\n\"]],[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[[],[[\"http://googleusercontent.com/youtube_content/1\"],null,null,null,[[[\"Is the three language formula actually working? | Focus Tamil Nadu\",\"vPTY8pRAPkA\",\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v\\u003dvPTY8pRAPkA\",\"The Hindu\",null,[\"Chapter 4 of Part I of the Report of the High-Level Committee on Union-State Relations deals with perhaps Tamil Nadu’s most sensitive issue — language. But this is not Tamil versus Hindi.  This is about what the Constitution actually says, whether Hindi numbers are inflated, whether English needs protection and whether India’s language policy needs a reset. It also challenges three assumptions: that Hindi is spoken by most Indians; migration makes Hindi essential, and the Three-Language Formula is working.\\n\\nThank you for watching our video!\\n\\nYou can subscribe to our channel here: https://bit.ly/3c8Adi6 \\n\\nVisit https://www.thehindu.com/ for the latest updates, analysis, opinions, and more. \\n\\nYou can also download our apps:\\nAndroid: https://bit.ly/3nboBEi\\niOs: https://apple.co/3EDCwt4\\n\\nThe Hindu is committed to keeping you up-to-date with information on the developments in India and the world. We promise to deliver quality journalism that stays away from vested interest and political propaganda. You can support us by subscribing to our digital offerings here: https://bit.ly/3emywiz \\n\\nFollow us: \\nTwitter: https://twitter.com/the_hindu \\nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/the_hindu/\"],null,18709,null,\"PT6M49S\",\"2026-02-26\",[\"c_e87b6c6ac16404a5_f1db0bcb-365f-42f6-85bc-e2ff0f3d3f99\"],\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/vPTY8pRAPkA/maxresdefault.jpg\"]]]]]]]]],null,\"56fdd199312815e2\",null,null,\"56fdd199312815e2\",null,null,null,\"3.8 Flash\",null,null,1,1],[1789201895,200332000]],[[\"c_e87b6c6ac16404a5\",\"r_510473f4292874f2\"],[\"c_e87b6c6ac16404a5\",\"r_52ec1746ed1f8d4f\",\"rc_06f0221f013644e0\"],[[\"What languages are chosen most under the NEP formula \",null,null,null,[[]]],2,null,1,\"56fdd199312815e2\",null,null,null,false,null,[]],[[[\"rc_40125598534228a6\",[\"While the NEP 2020 states that students and states have \\\"complete flexibility\\\" in selecting their three languages (as long as at least two are native Indian languages), ground-level curriculum choices and school board rollouts (such as CBSE and state boards) reveal clear patterns in what actually gets picked:\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 1. Hindi-Speaking States (UP, Bihar, MP, Rajasthan, Haryana, Delhi)\\nUnder the original intent of the 1968 and 2020 formulas, Hindi-speaking states were meant to pick a modern South Indian or other non-Hindi regional language (like Tamil, Telugu, or Bengali) as their third language. In reality, almost no schools do this.\\n\\n* **Language 1:** Hindi (Primary native language)\\n* **Language 2:** English (Global / Link language)\\n* **Language 3:** **Sanskrit** (Overwhelmingly dominant)\\n* **Alternative Third Languages:** Urdu (in pockets of UP, Bihar, and Delhi) or Punjabi (Haryana and Delhi).\\n\\n**Why Sanskrit dominates:** Sanskrit qualifies as a \\\"native Indian language\\\" under the Eighth Schedule, satisfying the NEP mandate. Because NCERT already has an established Sanskrit curriculum, grading rubrics, and teachers across northern government and private schools, schools universally default to it rather than investing in Malayalam or Kannada teachers.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2. Non-Hindi States (Southern, Western, and Eastern India)\\nIn states that follow the three-language framework (outside Tamil Nadu, which strictly retains its two-language model of Tamil + English):\\n\\n* **Language 1:** State Official Language (e.g., Kannada, Telugu, Malayalam, Marathi, Gujarati, Bengali, Odia)\\n* **Language 2:** English (Medium of instruction or primary link language)\\n* **Language 3:** **Hindi** (Overwhelmingly dominant)\\n* **Secondary Alternative for Language 3:** **Sanskrit**\\n\\n**Why Hindi dominates here:** Because English counts as the non-Indian language in the formula, the remaining two must be native Indian languages. Since Language 1 is the state language, the only other Indian language with widely available textbooks, Union-funded teachers, and board exam infrastructure is Hindi. Sanskrit is occasionally chosen in private CBSE schools as an alternative to avoid Hindi or because of perceived scoring advantages in board exams.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 3. Central \\u0026 Private Boards (CBSE, ICSE, Kendriya Vidyalayas)\\nBoards directly guided by central directives (CBSE, KVs, Navodaya Vidyalayas) heavily dictate these outcomes:\\n\\n* **The Phasing Out of Foreign Languages:** Previously, many urban students opted for French, German, or Spanish as their third language. Under the strict NEP rule that at least two languages must be native Indian languages, English + a foreign language leaves room for only one Indian language. As a result, CBSE has clamped down on foreign languages as the third subject, forcing students into **Sanskrit** or **Hindi**.\\n* **Kendriya Vidyalayas (KVs):** KVs across India (even in non-Hindi states) overwhelmingly offer **English, Hindi, and Sanskrit**. In Tamil Nadu, for instance, parliamentary records revealed that KVs employed scores of full-time Hindi and Sanskrit teachers compared to a handful of contractual Tamil teachers.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### In Practice: The De Facto Trio\\n\\nDespite the theoretical option to study 22 scheduled languages:\\n\\n| Student Cohort | De Facto Language 1 | De Facto Language 2 | De Facto Language 3 |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Northern / Hindi Heartland** | Hindi | English | **Sanskrit** (sometimes Urdu/Punjabi) |\\n| **Western / Eastern States** (MH, GJ, WB, OD) | Regional Language | English | **Hindi** (rarely Sanskrit) |\\n| **Southern States** (KA, AP, TS, KL) | Regional Language | English | **Hindi** (rarely Sanskrit) |\\n| **Tamil Nadu State Board** | Tamil (Strict 2-Language Policy) | English | *None (formula rejected)* |\\n\\nIn practical implementation across India, the \\\"multilingual menu\\\" collapses into a predictable loop between **State Language, English, Hindi, and Sanskrit**.\"],[null,[[[\"While the NEP 2020 states that students and states have \\\"complete flexibility\\\" in selecting their three languages (as long as at least two are native Indian languages), ground-level curriculum choices and school board rollouts (such as CBSE and state boards) reveal clear patterns in what actually gets picked:\",null,null,[[0,310],[0,310]]],[1],[[\"https://www.hindustantimes.com/india-news/74-students-enrolled-in-61-schools-offering-three-languages-govt-data-101742831827943.html#:~:text\\u003dThe%20minister's%20response%20came%20amid%20the%20ongoing,a%20two%2Dlanguage%20system%20of%20Tamil%20and%20English.\",\"74% students enrolled in 61% schools offe