[[null,null,null,null,2]]],[12,46,[null,null,null,[null,[\"spp_48e89a922824487d\"]]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"Alternative Third Languages: Urdu (in pockets of UP, Bihar, and Delhi) or Punjabi (Haryana and Delhi).\",[[0,28,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]]]]],[null,[null,0,\"Why Sanskrit dominates: Sanskrit qualifies as a \\\"native Indian language\\\" under the Eighth Schedule, satisfying the NEP mandate. Because NCERT already has an established Sanskrit curriculum, grading rubrics, and teachers across northern government and private schools, schools universally default to it rather than investing in Malayalam or Kannada teachers.\",[[0,23,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]],[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[]],[null,[null,3,\"2. Non-Hindi States (Southern, Western, and Eastern India)\"]],[null,[null,0,\"In states that follow the three-language framework (outside Tamil Nadu, which strictly retains its two-language model of Tamil + English):\",[[0,138,[null,null,null,[null,[\"spp_409172ea1c39401e\"]]]]]]],[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[[[[[null,[null,0,\"Language 1: State Official Language (e.g., Kannada, Telugu, Malayalam, Marathi, Gujarati, Bengali, Odia)\",[[0,11,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"Language 2: English (Medium of instruction or primary link language)\",[[0,11,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"Language 3: Hindi (Overwhelmingly dominant)\",[[0,11,[[null,null,null,null,2]]],[12,17,[[null,null,null,null,2]]],[12,43,[null,null,null,[null,[\"spp_fa544991ae634216\"]]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"Secondary Alternative for Language 3: Sanskrit\",[[0,37,[[null,null,null,null,2]]],[38,46,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]]]]],[null,[null,0,\"Why Hindi dominates here: Because English counts as the non-Indian language in the formula, the remaining two must be native Indian languages. Since Language 1 is the state language, the only other Indian language with widely available textbooks, Union-funded teachers, and board exam infrastructure is Hindi. Sanskrit is occasionally chosen in private CBSE schools as an alternative to avoid Hindi or because of perceived scoring advantages in board exams.\",[[0,25,[[null,null,null,null,2]]],[0,142,[null,null,null,[null,[\"spp_a1b49ef29f0a4bec\"]]]]]]],[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[]],[null,[null,3,\"3. Central \\u0026 Private Boards (CBSE, ICSE, Kendriya Vidyalayas)\"]],[null,[null,0,\"Boards directly guided by central directives (CBSE, KVs, Navodaya Vidyalayas) heavily dictate these outcomes:\"]],[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[[[[[null,[null,0,\"The Phasing Out of Foreign Languages: Previously, many urban students opted for French, German, or Spanish as their third language. Under the strict NEP rule that at least two languages must be native Indian languages, English + a foreign language leaves room for only one Indian language. As a result, CBSE has clamped down on foreign languages as the third subject, forcing students into Sanskrit or Hindi.\",[[0,37,[[null,null,null,null,2]]],[390,398,[[null,null,null,null,2]]],[402,407,[[null,null,null,null,2]]],[132,289,[null,null,null,[null,[\"spp_4b151dbb90cd4c34\"]]]],[290,408,[null,null,null,[null,[\"spp_637e1920cbbf4d9b\"]]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"Kendriya Vidyalayas (KVs): KVs across India (even in non-Hindi states) overwhelmingly offer English, Hindi, and Sanskrit. In Tamil Nadu, for instance, parliamentary records revealed that KVs employed scores of full-time Hindi and Sanskrit teachers compared to a handful of contractual Tamil teachers.\",[[0,26,[[null,null,null,null,2]]],[92,120,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]]]]],[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[]],[null,[null,3,\"In Practice: The De Facto Trio\"]],[null,[null,0,\"Despite the theoretical option to study 22 scheduled languages:\"]],[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[[[null,[[[[null,[null,0,\"Student Cohort\"]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"De Facto Language 1\"]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"De Facto Language 2\"]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"De Facto Language 3\"]]]]]],[null,[[[[null,[null,0,\"Northern / Hindi Heartland\",[[0,26,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"Hindi\"]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"English\"]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"Sanskrit (sometimes Urdu/Punjabi)\",[[0,8,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]]]],[null,[[[[null,[null,0,\"Western / Eastern States (MH, GJ, WB, OD)\",[[0,24,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"Regional Language\"]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"English\"]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"Hindi (rarely Sanskrit)\",[[0,5,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]]]],[null,[[[[null,[null,0,\"Southern States (KA, AP, TS, KL)\",[[0,15,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"Regional Language\"]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"English\"]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"Hindi (rarely Sanskrit)\",[[0,5,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]]]],[null,[[[[null,[null,0,\"Tamil Nadu State Board\",[[0,22,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"Tamil (Strict 2-Language Policy)\"]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"English\"]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"None (formula rejected)\",[[0,23,[[null,null,null,null,1]]]]]]]]]]],true]],[null,[null,0,\"In practical implementation across India, the \\\"multilingual menu\\\" collapses into a predictable loop between State Language, English, Hindi, and Sanskrit.\",[[108,152,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]]],null,\"56fdd199312815e2\",null,null,\"56fdd199312815e2\",null,null,null,\"3.8 Flash\",null,null,1,1],[1789201881,716926000]],[[\"c_e87b6c6ac16404a5\",\"r_52ec1746ed1f8d4f\"],[\"c_e87b6c6ac16404a5\",\"r_12890d546c20aba3\",\"rc_8a7f115f22a0479b\"],[[\"Well, Why RSS \\u0026 BJP is using NEP and forcing 3 language policy , which in practice is imposing Hindi in non Hindi states, despite the fact that many southern states are naturally speaking native languages , whereas RSS want to spread a language from Indo-Iranian branch to those states ? \",null,null,null,[[]]],2,null,1,\"56fdd199312815e2\",null,null,null,false,null,[]],[[[\"rc_06f0221f013644e0\",[\"The push for the three-language formula through policies like the National Education Policy (NEP) exposes the fundamental tension between a **centralized, homogenization-driven nation-state project** and India’s reality as a **multilingual, multinational federation**. \\n\\nTo the Sangh Parivar, linguistic taxonomy (Indo-Aryan vs. Dravidian) is secondary to an ideological vision: constructing a singular, cohesive national identity.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 1. The Ideological Blueprint: \\\"Hindi, Hindu, Hindustan\\\"\\nThe RSS’s foundational philosophy, derived from early ideologues like V.D. Savarkar and M.S. Golwalkar, views national strength through the European-derived 19th-century lens of cultural homogeneity: **one people, one culture, one language**. \\n\\n* **A Bridge to Sanskrit:** In the Hindutva framework, Sanskrit is the sacred civilizational core (*Devabhasha*). Standard Hindi (written in Devanagari and heavily Sanskritized) is treated as the modern, demographically viable proxy to unify the country under a Sanskritic cultural umbrella.\\n* **The \\\"Link Language\\\" Argument:** As articulated repeatedly by political leaders like Amit Shah, the BJP’s policy posture is that India needs an indigenous *lingua franca* rather than relying on English, which they frame as a colonial relic. Because Hindi has the largest demographic footprint in northern and central India, it is treated as the default national conduit.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2. How the NEP Formula Functions as Backdoor Imposition\\nOfficially, the NEP text emphasizes \\\"multilingual flexibility\\\" and states that no specific language will be imposed, mandating only that two of the three languages be native Indian languages. However, critics and state governments (notably Tamil Nadu) argue that in practice, the structural mechanics ensure Hindi becomes mandatory:\\n\\n* **The Two-Indian-Language Mandate:** Most non-Hindi states run on a two-language system: the state language + English. By requiring students to pick two native Indian languages plus an optional third (usually English), schools are compelled to find another Indian language.\\n* **Resource Asymmetry:** The Union government heavily subsidizes Hindi education (funding Hindi teachers, Hindi prachar sabhas, and central textbooks). A school in Madurai or Tiruchirappalli cannot easily hire teachers for Bengali, Odia, or Marathi. The only Indian language with readily available teachers, materials, and central funding is Hindi. The \\\"choice\\\" becomes an illusion.\\n* **Failure of Reciprocity:** The original 1968 Three-Language Formula mandated that northern states teach a South Indian language alongside Hindi and English. Instead, northern states almost universally adopted Sanskrit or Urdu as their \\\"third language,\\\" completely ignoring southern Dravidian tongues. The burden of learning an additional living language fell entirely onto non-Hindi speakers.\\n* **Fiscal Coercion:** The Centre has repeatedly conditioned federal educational funds (such as the *Samagra Shiksha Abhiyan*) on states adopting the NEP framework. Non-compliant states face funding cuts, turning language adoption into an ultimatum.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 3. Why Linguistics Doesn't Factor into Their Calculations\\nFrom a cladistic and historical linguistic standpoint, pushing an Indo-Aryan (Indo-European) language onto Dravidian-speaking populations makes no natural sense. The language families have distinct phonologies, grammars, roots, and evolutionary paths.\\n\\nHowever, the RSS rejects this linguistic separation:\\n* **Denial of the Aryan-Dravidian Division:** Hindutva historiography rejects the Indo-Aryan migration model and downplays Caldwell’s classification of Dravidian languages as an entirely distinct family. \\n* **The \\\"All Languages Evolved from Sanskrit\\\" Myth:** RSS-affiliated educational bodies consistently advance the claim that all Indian languages (including Tamil, Kannada, and Telugu) are derived from or heavily indebted to Sanskrit. Under this belief system, spreading Hindi or Sanskritized vocabulary to the South is not seen as foreign displacement, but as restoring a civilizational continuum.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 4. The Political \\u0026 Economic Stakes for the South\\nFor states like Tamil Nadu, rejecting the three-language formula is not just about phonetics—it is about **asymmetric disadvantage and federal autonomy**:\\n\\n| Dimension | The South's Reality (Two-Language System) | The Centralized Imposition Model |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Cognitive Load** | Students master the local mother tongue (Tamil) and the global link language (English). | Non-Hindi students must learn three languages, while native Hindi students effectively learn two (Hindi + English). |\\n| **Employment** | English provides access to the global knowledge economy, IT, and international trade. | Hindi provides zero distinct economic advantage outside regional northern markets. |\\n| **Public Sector Exams** | Southern aspirants face a structural handicap in Union civil services and banking exams where Hindi is heavily prioritized. | Native Hindi speakers gain an unfair competitive advantage in federal governance jobs. |\\n\\nBy using the NEP to embed Hindi nationwide, the BJP and RSS are attempting to build an administrative and cultural monoculture that eases political centralisation from Delhi. For non-Hindi states, defending the two-language policy is the primary defensive barrier preserving linguistic self-determination and genuine federalism.\"],[null,[[[\"The push for the three-language formula through policies like the National Education Policy (NEP) exposes the fundamental tension between a **centralized, homogenization-driven nation-state project** and India’s reality as a **multilingual, multinational federation**.\",null,null,[[0,270],[0,268]]],[1],[[\"https://sabrangindia.in/tamil-nadus-opposition-to-nep-2020s-three-language-formula-a-federal-pushback-against-central-imposition/#:~:text\\u003dTamil%20Nadu's%20opposition%20to%20NEP%202020's%20three%2Dlanguage,at%20times%2C%20has%20also%20posed%20policy%20challenges.\",\"Tamil Nadu's opposition to NEP 2020's three-language formula\",\"https://encrypted-tbn1.gstatic.com/favicon-tbn?q\\u003dtbn%3AANd9GcQ3mBsAA9ooZlLBcUIWKVNRBpuRGrQNXMTFBWszZjrimlXO-KbI1NIpTlKCoa6LGpkxg5-gOQoaDx2pIiNriwS6gEVobDdn81eN9w\",\"Tamil Nadu's opposition to NEP 2020's three-language formula: a federal \\npushback against central imposition * India's education system has long \\nbeen influenced ...\",[[\"The push for the three-language formula through policies like the National Education Policy (NEP) exposes the fundamental tension between a **centralized, homogenization-driven nation-state project** and India’s reality as a **multilingual, multinational federation**.\",null,null,[[0,270],[0,268]]]],null,\"Sabrang India\",9,\"\"]],\"spp_cc34e5efaaef4786\"],[[\"As articulated repeatedly by political leaders like Amit Shah, the BJP’s policy posture is that India needs an indigenous *lingua franca* rather than relying on English, which they frame as a colonial relic.\",null,null,[[1075,1284],[1071,1278]]],[1],[[\"https://m.thewire.in/article/education/understanding-why-the-three-language-formula-threatens-south-india#:~:text\\u003dDoes%20the%20Indian%20diaspora%20consist%20only%20of,for%20the%20states%20of%20the%20North%20East.\",\"Why the Three-Language Formula Threatens South India - The Wire\",\"https://encrypted-tbn1.gstatic.com/favicon-tbn?q\\u003dtbn%3AANd9GcSdSO30zok0GCfub90hzE9JdPxlYk56XL7bSJlq6EFecRwPWzjmQf--fyUFT1W0eLT5SiYarNWlOnMcW6_CwWPJj0t2ScvpWA\",\"Does the Indian diaspora consist only of Hindi speaking people? There are \\nmore examples of the bias of the BJP government in favour of Hindi. Union \\nhome ministe...\",[[\"As articulated repeatedly by poli