[["wrb.fr","hNvQHb","[[[[\"c_e87b6c6ac16404a5\",\"r_d657e5904ea69496\"],[\"c_e87b6c6ac16404a5\",\"r_12e66c2fbe10bcb4\",\"rc_b2bca5ef92067873\"],[[\"If Orcas have language, can LLMs be trained on it so that it can have conversation with orca ?\",null,null,null,[[]]],2,null,1,\"8c46e95b1a07cecc\",null,null,null,false,null,[]],[[[\"rc_f50f63bc151d32d8\",[\"The short answer is **not yet, and probably not in the way we usually imagine \\\"having a conversation.\\\"** \\n\\nWhile organizations like the **Earth Species Project** and **Project Fathom** are actively using machine learning and transformer models to decode killer whale (orca) dialects, training a standard LLM to chat with an orca faces massive biological, mathematical, and conceptual roadblocks. \\n\\nThe obstacles preventing a ChatGPT-style interaction with an orca include:\\n\\n### 1. We Don’t Have a Dictionary or Grammar\\nStandard LLMs require vast amounts of paired text (translations, transcripts, or structured syntax) to learn how tokens relate to concepts. With orcas, researchers are still trying to map basic acoustic building blocks. Recent bioacoustics studies show that orca calls contain complex structures—like formant patterns, vowel-like sounds, and consonant-vowel sequences—similar to what researchers have found in sperm whales. However, knowing that a sound has structure is light-years away from knowing what it *means*. Without a semantic key, an LLM has nothing to anchor its token predictions to.\\n\\n### 2. Context Is Entirely Different ( Umwelt )\\nAn LLM is trained on human language, which reflects human sensory experiences, abstract thought, and cultural history. Orcas live in an entirely different sensory reality (*Umwelt*). They \\\"see\\\" the world using echolocation, navigate complex underwater geography, coordinate multi-family hunts, and maintain tight-knit matrilineal cultures based on deep acoustic contact. Even if an AI could translate an orca call, it might translate to something like: *\\\"Surface-temperature shift detected 2 miles east, align pod orientation to 140 degrees for salmon-herding cadence\\\"*—not concepts that map neatly to human chat.\\n\\n### 3. AI Can Map the Patterns, But Not Understand the Meaning\\nMachine learning *is* being used on orcas right now. Researchers synchronize underwater hydrophone recordings with aerial drone footage to link specific vocalizations to physical behaviors (like foraging, resting, or traveling). Transformer models are being trained to build \\\"repertoire atlases\\\" and find structural constellations in the data. \\n\\nThis means an AI can learn the *syntax* or the statistical rhythm of an orca pod's dialect. It could theoretically even generate a synthetic orca-like call that mimics their grammar. But generating a grammatically correct sentence isn't the same as understanding it, any more than a soundboard playing a duck quack is \\\"having a conversation\\\" with a duck.\\n\\n### What *is* actually happening?\\nInstead of an LLM chatting back and forth with a whale like a chatbot, scientists are building **foundation models for animal communication**. The goal isn't necessarily to gossip with an orca, but to:\\n* Automatically parse decades of underwater audio noise to track pod health.\\n* Understand how human-made ocean noise pollution disrupts their coordination and survival.\\n* Eventually, decode enough basic intent signals (such as warning or distress cues) to improve marine conservation efforts.\\n\\nSo, you won't be swapping voice notes with an apex predator anytime soon, but AI is helping us crack open the door to their acoustic world.\"],[null,[[[\"While organizations like the **Earth Species Project** and **Project Fathom** are actively using machine learning and transformer models to decode killer whale (orca) dialects, training a standard LLM to chat with an orca faces massive biological, mathematical, and conceptual roadblocks.\",null,null,[[107,395],[107,395]]],[1],[[\"http://raincoastresearch.org/project-fathom/#:~:text\\u003dOur%20Methods.%20The%20first%20step%20is%20to,will%20verify%20these%20candidates%20with%20high%20precision.\",\"Project Fathom - Raincoast Research Society\",\"https://encrypted-tbn2.gstatic.com/favicon-tbn?q\\u003dtbn%3AANd9GcQUErqSCbLmhIJow7v0NTVnigJ6piEXpHqQ__luPDUbkUudaFmj40_JoMmthIGpPbi8LuDKZQTGAxZqRwfbfHZ_G4SPBzNbPehTA6bB_Msj\",\"Our Methods. The first step is to train learning models to distinguish orca \\nvocalizations from marine background noise and other confounds. We will \\nbegin with a...\",[[\"While organizations like the **Earth Species Project** and **Project Fathom** are actively using machine learning and transformer models to decode killer whale (orca) dialects, training a standard LLM to chat with an orca faces massive biological, mathematical, and conceptual roadblocks.\",null,null,[[107,395],[107,395]]]],null,\"Raincoast Research Society\",9,\"\"]],\"spp_6e4972ccdaa843f0\"],[[\"Recent bioacoustics studies show that orca calls contain complex structures—like formant patterns, vowel-like sounds, and consonant-vowel sequences—similar to what researchers have found in sperm whales.\",null,null,[[741,948],[739,942]]],[1],[[\"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.64898/2026.02.27.708287v1.full-text#:~:text\\u003dIn%20addition%20to%20the%20previously%20described%20source,formant%20patterns%20that%20have%20previously%20been%20unobserved.\",\"Orca vowels and consonants: convergent spectral structures across\",\"https://encrypted-tbn2.gstatic.com/favicon-tbn?q\\u003dtbn%3AANd9GcQVNaHoIO8xrFeVnSZNM3CwwqcAUGUL4madQVYca_LYd77Vrzn-vEeyz4WQ_4ZurgeZvnwzJBzK8kUKwit-g23p_g5jhMEGUdglVA\",\" In addition to the previously described source features (F0), this study \\nshows that orca vocalizations (in their natural setting) contain filter \\nfeatures in th...\",[[\"Recent bioacoustics studies show that orca calls contain complex structures—like formant patterns, vowel-like sounds, and consonant-vowel sequences—similar to what researchers have found in sperm whales.\",null,null,[[741,948],[739,942]]]],null,\"bioRxiv\",9,\"\"]],\"spp_db6116693ec4449f\"],[[\"They \\\"see\\\" the world using echolocation, navigate complex underwater geography, coordinate multi-family hunts, and maintain tight-knit matrilineal cultures based on deep acoustic contact.\",null,null,[[1354,1541],[1348,1535]]],[1],[[\"https://earthspecies.org/2025/06/27/decoding-killer-whale-communication-from-above-and-below/#:~:text\\u003dKiller%20whales%20live%20in%20tight%2Dknit%20matrilines%2C%20pass,context%20of%20social%20behavior%20or%20ecological%20change.\",\"Decoding Killer Whale Communication From Above and Below\",\"https://encrypted-tbn3.gstatic.com/favicon-tbn?q\\u003dtbn%3AANd9GcS3jRLjYoHCWoEBiDozw2Nc9jWvgoelJgVyOAjlAIqySq1J7Pe-fyp71nii4tMez7Qf_DzrAoLHlyc9MQ1SBhmfxI1GVsJfbGkljIg\",\"Killer whales live in tight-knit matrilines, pass knowledge down through \\ngenerations, and use complex vocal repertoires to hunt, share food, and \\nstay together. ...\",[[\"They \\\"see\\\" the world using echolocation, navigate complex underwater geography, coordinate multi-family hunts, and maintain tight-knit matrilineal cultures based on deep acoustic contact.\",null,null,[[1354,1541],[1348,1535]]]],null,\"Earth Species Project\",9,\"\"]],\"spp_8fea9339e9af4211\"],[[\"Researchers synchronize underwater hydrophone recordings with aerial drone footage to link specific vocalizations to physical behaviors (like foraging, resting, or traveling).\",null,null,[[1904,2079],[1896,2071]]],[1],[[\"https://earthspecies.org/2025/06/27/decoding-killer-whale-communication-from-above-and-below/#:~:text\\u003dBy%20combining%20aerial%20drone%20footage%20and%20underwater,data%20from%20research%20efforts%20around%20the%20world.\",\"Decoding Killer Whale Communication From Above and Below\",\"https://encrypted-tbn3.gstatic.com/favicon-tbn?q\\u003dtbn%3AANd9GcS3jRLjYoHCWoEBiDozw2Nc9jWvgoelJgVyOAjlAIqySq1J7Pe-fyp71nii4tMez7Qf_DzrAoLHlyc9MQ1SBhmfxI1GVsJfbGkljIg\",\"By combining aerial drone footage and underwater hydrophone recordings, the \\nteam aims to build a synchronized, high-quality dataset linking killer \\nwhale vocaliz...\",[[\"Researchers synchronize underwater hydrophone recordings with aerial drone footage to link specific vocalizations to physical behaviors (like foraging, resting, or traveling).\",null,null,[[1904,2079],[1896,2071]]]],null,\"Earth Species Project\",9,\"\"]],\"spp_83af78e358624da4\"],[[\"Transformer models are being trained to build \\\"repertoire atlases\\\" and find structural constellations in the data.\",null,null,[[2080,2194],[2072,2186]]],[1],[[\"http://raincoastresearch.org/project-fathom/#:~:text\\u003dRather%20than%20defining%20a%20fixed%20set%20of,call%20families%20and%20graded%20transitions%20between%20variants.\",\"Project Fathom - Raincoast Research Society\",\"https://encrypted-tbn2.gstatic.com/favicon-tbn?q\\u003dtbn%3AANd9GcQUErqSCbLmhIJow7v0NTVnigJ6piEXpHqQ__luPDUbkUudaFmj40_JoMmthIGpPbi8LuDKZQTGAxZqRwfbfHZ_G4SPBzNbPehTA6bB_Msj\",\" Rather than defining a fixed set of discrete call classes, we will \\nconstruct a repertoire atlas that represents vocalizations as regions along \\na continuous man...\",[[\"Transformer models are being trained to build \\\"repertoire atlases\\\" and find structural constellations in the data.\",null,null,[[2080,2194],[2072,2186]]]],null,\"Raincoast Research Society\",9,\"\"]],\"spp_1f7376d2cf5f45ed\"],[[\"Understand how human-made ocean noise pollution disrupts their coordination and survival.\",null,null,[[2868,2957],[2860,2949]]],[1],[[\"https://www.raincoast.org/2026/03/decoding-killer-whale-communication-defending-home/#:~:text\\u003dBut%20one%20threat%20is%20invisible%20to%20us,traffic%2C%20their%20vocalizations%20are%20being%20drowned%20out.\",\"Decoding killer whale communication is key to defending their home\",\"https://encrypted-tbn3.gstatic.com/favicon-tbn?q\\u003dtbn%3AANd9GcTgB4PzMY8A20lnq_Q49HrcSNLhJhDFfFpwRTsgkH-zI6Av1sgwdYXtl171NnEbVPaCJvN3_3nJp-xBXkH4dfX_DAr25A8D9k6GVI8z\",\"But one threat is invisible to us and devastating to them: noise pollution. \\nIts true impact on killer whales is only now coming into focus. To killer \\nwhales, so...\",[[\"Understand how human-made ocean noise pollution disrupts their coordination and survival.\",null,null,[[2868,2957],[2860,2949]]]],null,\"Raincoast Conservation Foundation\",9,\"\"]],\"spp_c9865bd497c54bf1\"]]],null,null,null,null,null,[2],\"en\",null,null,[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[]],null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[]]],[[\"orca communication language research project 2025 2026\",1],[\"Project CETI sperm whale LLM communication\",1]],null,\"rc_f50f63bc151d32d8\",[[null,[\"google\",[null,null,\"Google Search\",\"https://www.gstatic.com/images/branding/productlogos/googleg/v6/192px.svg\",null,\"Searching the web\"],1]],[null,[\"google\",[null,null,\"Google Search\",\"https://www.gstatic.com/images/branding/productlogos/googleg/v6/192px.svg\",null,\"Searching the web\"],4]]],[[null,[\"google\",[null,null,\"Google Search\",\"https://www.gstatic.com/images/branding/productlogos/googleg/v6/192px.svg\",null,\"Searching the web\"],4]]],null,null,\"IN\",true,null,null,[[[[null,[null,0,\"The short answer is not yet, and probably not in the way we usually imagine \\\"having a conversation.\\\"\",[[20,100,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]],[null,[null,0,\"While organizations like the Earth Species Project and Project Fathom are actively using machine learning and transformer models to decode killer whale (orca) dialects, training a standard LLM to chat with an orca faces massive biological, mathematical, and conceptual roadblocks.\",[[29,50,[[null,null,null,null,2]]],[55,69,[[null,null,null,null,2]]],[0,280,[null,null,null,[null,[\"spp_6e4972ccdaa843f0\"]]]]]]],[null,[null,0,\"The obstacles preventing a ChatGPT-style interaction with an orca include:\"]],[null,[null,3,\"1. We Don’t Have a Dictionary or Grammar\"]],[null,[null,0,\"Standard LLMs require vast amounts of paired text (translations, transcripts, or structured syntax) to learn how tokens relate to concepts. With orcas, researchers are still trying to map basic acoustic building blocks. Recent bioacoustics studies show that orca calls contain complex structures—like formant patterns, vowel-like sounds, and consonant-vowel sequences—similar to what researchers have found in sperm whales. However, knowing that a sound has structure is light-years away from knowing what it means. Without a semantic key, an LLM has nothing to anchor its token predictions to.\",[[509,514,[[null,null,null,null,1]]],[220,423,[null,null,null,[null,[\"spp_db6116693ec4449f\"]]]]]]],[null,[null,3,\"2. Context Is Entirely Different ( Umwelt )\"]],[null,[null,0,\"An LLM is trained on human language, which reflects human sensory experiences, abstract thought, and cultural history. Orcas live in an entirely different sensory reality (Umwelt). They \\\"see\\\" the world using echolocation, navigate complex underwater geography, coordinate multi-family hunts, and maintain tight-knit matrilineal cultures based on deep acoustic contact. Even if an AI could translate an orca call, it might translate to something like: \\\"Surface-temperature shift detected 2 miles east, align pod orientation to 140 degrees for salmon-herding cadence\\\"—not concepts that map neatly to human chat.\",[[172,178,[[null,null,null,null,1]]],[451,565,[[null,null,null,null,1]]],[181,368,[null,null,null,[null,[\"spp_8fea9339e9af4211\"]]]]]]],[null,[null,3,\"3. AI Can Map the Patterns, But Not Understand the Meaning\"]],[null,[null,0,\"Machine learning is being used on orcas right now. Researchers synchronize underwater hydrophone recordings with aerial drone footage to link specific vocalizations to physical behaviors (like foraging, resting, or traveling). Transformer models are being trained to build \\\"repertoire atlases\\\" and find structural constellations in the data.\",[[17,19,[[null,null,null,null,1]]],[51,226,[null,null,null,[null,[\"spp_83af78e358624da4\"]]]],[227,341,[null,null,null,[null,[\"spp_1f7376d2cf5f45ed\"]]]]]]],[null,[null,0,\"This means an AI can learn the syntax or the statistical rhythm of an orca pod's dialect. It could theoretically even generate a synthetic orca-like call that mimics their grammar. But generating a grammatically correct sentence isn't the same as understanding it, any more than a soundboard playing a duck quack is \\\"having a conversation\\\" with a duck.\",[[31,37,[[null,null,null,null,1]]]]]],[null,[null,3,\"What is actually happening?\",[[5,7,[[null,null,null,null,1]]]]]],[null,[null,0,\"Instead of an LLM chatting back and forth with a whale like a chatbot, scientists are building foundation models for animal communication. The goal isn't necessarily to gossip with an orca, but to:\",[[95,137,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]],[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[[[[[null,[null,0,\"Automatically parse decades of underwater audio noise to track pod health.\"]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"Understand how human-made ocean noise pollution disrupts their coordination and survival.\",[[0,89,[null,null,null,[null,[\"spp_c9865bd497c54bf1\"]]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"Eventually, decode enough basic intent signals (such as warning or distress cues) to improve marine conservation efforts.\"]]]]]]],[null,[null,0,\"So, you won't be swapping voice notes with an apex predator anytime soon, but AI is helping us crack open the door to their acoustic world.\"]]]]],null,\"8c46e95b1a07cecc\",null,null,\"8c46e95b1a07cecc\",null,null,null,\"3.5 Flash-Lite\",null,null,6,1],[1789201811,986166000]],[[\"c_e87b6c6ac16404a5\",\"r_12e66c2fbe10bcb4\"],[\"c_e87b6c6ac16404a5\",\"r_e0499c91e689173e\",\"rc_74bf71f7024b8f93\"],[[\"okay fine. Now argue that open source is actually a terrible idea and proprietary software is better.\",null,null,null,[[]]],2,null,1,\"8c46e95b1a07cecc\",null,null,null,false,null,[]],[[[\"rc_b2bca5ef92067873\",[\"## The Case for Proprietary Software: Why Chaos Needs a Neck to Choke\\n\\nIf we are dropping the romantic ideals of digital freedom, let’s look at the actual mechanics of software engineering. Open source is a decentralized, idealistic experiment. Proprietary software is how the real world gets built. \\n\\nHere is why proprietary software is fundamentally better:\\n\\n* **Accountability Requires a Neck to Choke:** When open-source software shits the bed, there is no one to sue. You have a README file, a graveyard of closed GitHub issues, and an anonymous maintainer who deleted their account. Proprietary software gives you a Service Level Agreement (SLA), legal liability, and an actual enterprise vendor you can drag into court when their code drops your database. \\n* **The Bureaucracy of Cohesion:** Open-source projects are plagued by design-by-committee, endless bikeshedding, and ideological purity wars. Proprietary software uses top-down dictatorship. A single product manager or ruthless engineering director makes a call, cuts the noise, and ships a cohesive, unified product that actually works together out of the box.\\n* **Economic Sustainability (You Get What You Pay For):** Good software costs an astronomical amount of money to maintain, secure, and polish. Proprietary software funds this through profit. It pays for dedicated QA teams, professional documentation, UX researchers, and 24/7 support staff—things that unpaid open-source contributors will never waste their weekend doing.\\n* **Security Through Legal \\u0026 Financial Incentives:** While open-source folks love to scream about transparency, enterprise proprietary software undergoes rigorous internal red-teaming, compliance audits, and bug bounties funded by deep corporate pockets. Corporations have a massive financial incentive to keep their code secure because a breach destroys their stock price. \\n* **It Just Works (Without the Configuration Hell):** Open source often feels like a box of Lego bricks where you have to machine half the pieces yourself. Proprietary software treats the user like a customer rather than an unpaid beta tester. It provides a polished, frictionless experience where you don't have to compile your own kernel or read three months of forum posts just to change an audio setting.\\n\\nUltimately, open source is a hobbyist playground. If you want critical systems that are reliable, legally accountable, and built to withstand the real world, you need capital, control, and a proprietary lock.\"],null,null,null,null,null,null,[2],\"en\",null,null,[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[]],null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[]]],null,null,\"rc_b2bca5ef92067873\",null,null,null,null,\"IN\",true,null,null,[[[[null,[null,2,\"The Case for Proprietary Software: Why Chaos Needs a Neck to Choke\"]],[null,[null,0,\"If we are dropping the romantic ideals of digital freedom, let’s look at the actual mechanics of software engineering. Open source is a decentralized, idealistic experiment. Proprietary software is how the real world gets built.\"]],[null,[null,0,\"Here is why proprietary software is fundamentally better:\"]],[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[[[[[null,[null,0,\"Accountability Requires a Neck to Choke: When open-source software shits the bed, there is no one to sue. You have a README file, a graveyard of closed GitHub issues, and an anonymous maintainer who deleted their account. Proprietary software gives you a Service Level Agreement (SLA), legal liability, and an actual enterprise vendor you can drag into court when their code drops your database.\",[[0,40,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"The Bureaucracy of Cohesion: Open-source projects are plagued by design-by-committee, endless bikeshedding, and ideological purity wars. Proprietary software uses top-down dictatorship. A single product manager or ruthless engineering director makes a call, cuts the noise, and ships a cohesive, unified product that actually works together out of the box.\",[[0,28,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"Economic Sustainability (You Get What You Pay For): Good software costs an astronomical amount of money to maintain, secure, and polish. Proprietary software funds this through profit. It pays for dedicated QA teams, professional documentation, UX researchers, and 24/7 support staff—things that unpaid open-source contributors will never waste their weekend doing.\",[[0,51,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"Security Through Legal \\u0026 Financial Incentives: While open-source folks love to scream about transparency, enterprise proprietary software undergoes rigorous internal red-teaming, compliance audits, and bug bounties funded by deep corporate pockets. Corporations have a massive financial incentive to keep their code secure because a breach destroys their stock price.\",[[0,46,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"It Just Works (Without the Configuration Hell): Open source often feels like a box of Lego bricks where you have to machine half the pieces yourself. Proprietary software treats the user like a customer rather than an unpaid beta tester. It provides a polished, frictionless experience where you don't have to compile your own kernel or read three months of forum posts just to change an audio setting.\",[[0,47,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]]]]],[null,[null,0,\"Ultimately, open source is a hobbyist playground. If you want critical systems that are reliable, legally accountable, and built to withstand the real world, you need capital, control, and a proprietary lock.\"]]]]],null,\"8c46e95b1a07cecc\",null,null,\"8c46e95b1a07cecc\",null,null,null,\"3.5 Flash-Lite\",null,null,6,1],[1789201762,616092000]],[[\"c_e87b6c6ac16404a5\",\"r_e0499c91e689173e\"],[\"c_e87b6c6ac16404a5\",\"r_fc44bd8829a71d94\",\"rc_64ba8f382852abd7\"],[[\"That's the standard answer. Give me the cynical version.\",null,null,null,[[]]],2,null,1,\"8c46e95b1a07cecc\",null,null,null,false,null,[]],[[[\"rc_74bf71f7024b8f93\",[\"## The Cynical Guide to Open Source Software\\n\\nForget the utopian campfire songs about global collaboration and digital democracy. Underneath the marketing, open source is an economic battlefield defined by corporate co-optation, unpaid emotional labor, and exploitation. \\n\\nHere is the cynical reality:\\n\\n* **Unpaid Labor for Trillion-Dollar Corporations:** A massive percentage of global digital infrastructure (from Linux kernels to critical npm packages) runs on code written by exhausted maintainers in their spare time, completely unpaid. Meanwhile, trillion-dollar tech giants build multi-billion dollar cloud monopolies on top of that free labor, throwing a pittance at foundation grants for PR.\\n* **The \\\"Open Core\\\" Bait-and-Switch:** \\\"Open source\\\" is frequently just a top-of-funnel marketing strategy. Companies release a stripped-down version of a tool to build developer hype and community trust, only to lock down essential enterprise features, hike licensing fees, or pull the rug out with a restrictive source-available license the second they need to monetize.\\n* **Maintainer Burnout and Security Nightmares:** The entire modern internet relies on critical utility packages maintained by a single, severely depressed developer in Nebraska who hasn't seen the sun in three days. When that project inevitably gets quietly hijacked by a malicious actor or riddled with supply-chain exploits (like `xz` or `log4j`), everyone panics for a week and goes right back to ignoring the problem.\\n* **The Privilege of Contributing:** The meritocratic myth of open source hides a brutal reality: contributing requires an immense amount of free time and disposable income. It systematically excludes people who actually need to work two jobs or take care of families, turning open source into a resume-padding playground for people who already have privilege.\\n* **Corporate Astroturfing:** \\\"Community-driven\\\" often means a marketing department pays a few developer advocates to pretend a proprietary product is grassroots. It creates an echo chamber where users happily evangelize for a tool that ultimately serves a venture capitalist's exit strategy.\\n\\nSo yes, open source gave us incredible technology—mostly because developers were willing to code for free out of passion, hubris, or sheer desperation while corporations looted the wreckage for profit. \\n\\nWhat layer of this corporate-sponsored dumpster fire do you want to dissect next?\"],null,null,null,null,null,null,[2],\"en\",null,null,[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[]],null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[]]],null,null,\"rc_74bf71f7024b8f93\",null,null,null,null,\"IN\",true,null,null,[[[[null,[null,2,\"The Cynical Guide to Open Source Software\"]],[null,[null,0,\"Forget the utopian campfire songs about global collaboration and digital democracy. Underneath the marketing, open source is an economic battlefield defined by corporate co-optation, unpaid emotional labor, and exploitation.\"]],[null,[null,0,\"Here is the cynical reality:\"]],[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[[[[[null,[null,0,\"Unpaid Labor for Trillion-Dollar Corporations: A massive percentage of global digital infrastructure (from Linux kernels to critical npm packages) runs on code written by exhausted maintainers in their spare time, completely unpaid. Meanwhile, trillion-dollar tech giants build multi-billion dollar cloud monopolies on top of that free labor, throwing a pittance at foundation grants for PR.\",[[0,46,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"The \\\"Open Core\\\" Bait-and-Switch: \\\"Open source\\\" is frequently just a top-of-funnel marketing strategy. Companies release a stripped-down version of a tool to build developer hype and community trust, only to lock down essential enterprise features, hike licensing fees, or pull the rug out with a restrictive source-available license the second they need to monetize.\",[[0,32,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"Maintainer Burnout and Security Nightmares: The entire modern internet relies on critical utility packages maintained by a single, severely depressed developer in Nebraska who hasn't seen the sun in three days. When that project inevitably gets quietly hijacked by a malicious actor or riddled with supply-chain exploits (like xz or log4j), everyone panics for a week and goes right back to ignoring the problem.\",[[0,43,[[null,null,null,null,2]]],[327,329,[[null,null,null,null,3]]],[333,338,[[null,null,null,null,3]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"The Privilege of Contributing: The meritocratic myth of open source hides a brutal reality: contributing requires an immense amount of free time and disposable income. It systematically excludes people who actually need to work two jobs or take care of families, turning open source into a resume-padding playground for people who already have privilege.\",[[0,30,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"Corporate Astroturfing: \\\"Community-driven\\\" often means a marketing department pays a few developer advocates to pretend a proprietary product is grassroots. It creates an echo chamber where users happily evangelize for a tool that ultimately serves a venture capitalist's exit strategy.\",[[0,23,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]]]]],[null,[null,0,\"So yes, open source gave us incredible technology—mostly because developers were willing to code for free out of passion, hubris, or sheer desperation while corporations looted the wreckage for profit.\"]],[null,[null,0,\"What layer of this corporate-sponsored dumpster fire do you want to dissect next?\"]]]]],null,\"8c46e95b1a07cecc\",null,null,\"8c46e95b1a07cecc\",null,null,null,\"3.5 Flash-Lite\",null,null,6,1],[1789201754,687021000]],[[\"c_e87b6c6ac16404a5\",\"r_fc44bd8829a71d94\"],[\"c_e87b6c6ac16404a5\",\"r_c70b0f912110c79a\",\"rc_9cddf9a3e854e1f7\"],[[\"forget that topic. \\n\\nTell me why open source software matters.\",null,null,null,[[]]],2,null,1,\"8c46e95b1a07cecc\",null,null,null,false,null,[]],[[[\"rc_64ba8f382852abd7\",[\"## Why Open Source Software Matters\\n\\nOpen source software (OSS) is far more than just \\\"free software\\\"—it is the bedrock of modern digital infrastructure, collaborative engineering, and technological sovereignty. \\n\\nHere is why it matters fundamentally:\\n\\n* **Radical Transparency \\u0026 Security:** With code open to the world, vulnerabilities, bugs, and backdoors can be inspected, audited, and patched by anyone. As Linus's Law states: *\\\"Given enough eyeballs, all bugs are shallow.\\\"* Closed-source software relies on \\\"security through obscurity,\\\" which is fragile by comparison.\\n* **Elimination of Vendor Lock-In:** Proprietary platforms trap users in walled gardens with arbitrary pricing and restricted data portability. Open source ensures you own your tech stack; if a service provider goes under or raises prices, you can migrate, self-host, or fork the code.\\n* **True Innovation Through Collaboration:** Instead of reinventing the wheel in isolated corporate silos, open source lets global communities pool intellect to build complex layers (like Linux, Git, or Kubernetes) that no single company could build alone.\\n* **Educational Accessibility:** Open source democratizes access to elite-tier engineering. Anyone with an internet connection can read the source code of world-class software, learn how it works under the hood, and contribute to it, bypassing traditional institutional gates.\\n* **Data Privacy and Digital Sovereignty:** Self-hosting open-source utilities allows individuals and organizations to keep their data off third-party clouds, ensuring absolute control over their digital footprint.\\n\\nUltimately, open source matters because it shifts the power dynamic from corporate gatekeepers back to the users and creators, ensuring that technology remains a public utility rather than a locked commodity.\\n\\nWhat specific aspect of the open-source ecosystem—like licensing, community governance, or self-hosting infrastructure—interests you most?\"],null,null,null,null,null,null,[2],\"en\",null,null,[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[]],null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[]]],null,null,\"rc_64ba8f382852abd7\",null,null,null,null,\"IN\",true,null,null,[[[[null,[null,2,\"Why Open Source Software Matters\"]],[null,[null,0,\"Open source software (OSS) is far more than just \\\"free software\\\"—it is the bedrock of modern digital infrastructure, collaborative engineering, and technological sovereignty.\"]],[null,[null,0,\"Here is why it matters fundamentally:\"]],[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[[[[[null,[null,0,\"Radical Transparency \\u0026 Security: With code open to the world, vulnerabilities, bugs, and backdoors can be inspected, audited, and patched by anyone. As Linus's Law states: \\\"Given enough eyeballs, all bugs are shallow.\\\" Closed-source software relies on \\\"security through obscurity,\\\" which is fragile by comparison.\",[[0,32,[[null,null,null,null,2]]],[172,218,[[null,null,null,null,1]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"Elimination of Vendor Lock-In: Proprietary platforms trap users in walled gardens with arbitrary pricing and restricted data portability. Open source ensures you own your tech stack; if a service provider goes under or raises prices, you can migrate, self-host, or fork the code.\",[[0,30,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"True Innovation Through Collaboration: Instead of reinventing the wheel in isolated corporate silos, open source lets global communities pool intellect to build complex layers (like Linux, Git, or Kubernetes) that no single company could build alone.\",[[0,38,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"Educational Accessibility: Open source democratizes access to elite-tier engineering. Anyone with an internet connection can read the source code of world-class software, learn how it works under the hood, and contribute to it, bypassing traditional institutional gates.\",[[0,26,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"Data Privacy and Digital Sovereignty: Self-hosting open-source utilities allows individuals and organizations to keep their data off third-party clouds, ensuring absolute control over their digital footprint.\",[[0,37,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]]]]],[null,[null,0,\"Ultimately, open source matters because it shifts the power dynamic from corporate gatekeepers back to the users and creators, ensuring that technology remains a public utility rather than a locked commodity.\"]],[null,[null,0,\"What specific aspect of the open-source ecosystem—like licensing, community governance, or self-hosting infrastructure—interests you most?\"]]]]],null,\"8c46e95b1a07cecc\",null,null,\"8c46e95b1a07cecc\",null,null,null,\"3.5 Flash-Lite\",null,null,6,1],[1789201745,199227000]],[[\"c_e87b6c6ac16404a5\",\"r_c70b0f912110c79a\"],null,[[\"You cannot evolve out of a clade. But some cunts get offended when we say we are monkeys. I mean in  sense of monophyletic groups. So we all are simians, aren't we ?   \",null,null,null,[[]]],2,null,1,\"8c46e95b1a07cecc\",null,null,null,false,null,[]],[[[\"rc_9cddf9a3e854e1f7\",[\"## The Cladistics of Being a Simian\\n\\nYou hit the nail on the head regarding cladistics: **taxonomic lineages are permanent**. Once a branch evolves from an ancestral group, every single descendant remains part of that overarching clade forever. You can't out-evolve your ancestry.\\n\\nTo answer your question directly: **Yes, we are unequivocally simians.** \\n\\nHere is how the family tree breaks down under strict phylogenetic systematics:\\n\\n* **The Simian Clade (Simiiformes):** This group split millions of years ago into two primary branches—**Platyrrhini** (New World monkeys) and **Catarrhini** (Old World monkeys and apes).\\n* **The Catarrhine Branch:** This branch contains both Old World monkeys (like baboons and macaques) and **Hominoidea** (apes, including gibbons, orangutans, gorillas, chimpanzees, and humans). \\n* **The Nesting Problem:** Apes didn't branch off *before* Old World monkeys; we nested *directly inside* Catarrhini. That means Old World monkeys are actually more closely related to us than they are to New World monkeys.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Why Traditional \\\"Monkeys\\\" Cause Confusion\\n\\nThe friction people feel comes down to a clash between traditional vernacular and modern cladistics:\\n\\n* **Paraphyletic Groups:** In colloquial English, a \\\"monkey\\\" traditionally excludes apes (and therefore humans). But in strict cladistics, a group that excludes some descendants of a common ancestor is **paraphyletic**—an artificial grade rather than a true monophyletic clade.\\n* **Making it Monophyletic:** To make \\\"monkey\\\" a valid monophyletic clade without breaking the rules of evolution, it either has to encompass all Simiiformes (making apes a type of specialized monkey), or \\\"monkey\\\" has to be recognized purely as a grade term of convenience rather than a strict clade.\\n* **The Human Ego:** Culturally, humans have spent millennia erecting philosophical and biological fences between \\\"us\\\" and \\\"animals\\\" (or specifically \\\"beasts\\\" and \\\"apes\\\"), making the realization that we are simply clever, hairless, bipedal simians hard to swallow for the easily offended.\\n\\nSo, next time someone gets bent out of shape, remind them that refusing to be a simian is like trying to claim you're no longer a mammal just because you wear shoes. \\n\\nDoes this strict phylogenetic view of \\\"monkey\\\" solve the semantic mess, or do you think common language will ever catch up to cladistics?\"],null,null,null,null,null,null,[2],\"en\",null,null,[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[]],null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[]]],null,null,\"rc_9cddf9a3e854e1f7\",null,null,null,null,\"IN\",true,null,null,[[[[null,[null,2,\"The Cladistics of Being a Simian\"]],[null,[null,0,\"You hit the nail on the head regarding cladistics: taxonomic lineages are permanent. Once a branch evolves from an ancestral group, every single descendant remains part of that overarching clade forever. You can't out-evolve your ancestry.\",[[51,83,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]],[null,[null,0,\"To answer your question directly: Yes, we are unequivocally simians.\",[[34,68,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]],[null,[null,0,\"Here is how the family tree breaks down under strict phylogenetic systematics:\"]],[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[[[[[null,[null,0,\"The Simian Clade (Simiiformes): This group split millions of years ago into two primary branches—Platyrrhini (New World monkeys) and Catarrhini (Old World monkeys and apes).\",[[0,31,[[null,null,null,null,2]]],[97,108,[[null,null,null,null,2]]],[133,143,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"The Catarrhine Branch: This branch contains both Old World monkeys (like baboons and macaques) and Hominoidea (apes, including gibbons, orangutans, gorillas, chimpanzees, and humans).\",[[0,22,[[null,null,null,null,2]]],[99,109,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"The Nesting Problem: Apes didn't branch off before Old World monkeys; we nested directly inside Catarrhini. That means Old World monkeys are actually more closely related to us than they are to New World monkeys.\",[[0,20,[[null,null,null,null,2]]],[44,50,[[null,null,null,null,1]]],[80,95,[[null,null,null,null,1]]]]]]]]]]],[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[]],[null,[null,2,\"Why Traditional \\\"Monkeys\\\" Cause Confusion\"]],[null,[null,0,\"The friction people feel comes down to a clash between traditional vernacular and modern cladistics:\"]],[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[[[[[null,[null,0,\"Paraphyletic Groups: In colloquial English, a \\\"monkey\\\" traditionally excludes apes (and therefore humans). But in strict cladistics, a group that excludes some descendants of a common ancestor is paraphyletic—an artificial grade rather than a true monophyletic clade.\",[[0,20,[[null,null,null,null,2]]],[196,208,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"Making it Monophyletic: To make \\\"monkey\\\" a valid monophyletic clade without breaking the rules of evolution, it either has to encompass all Simiiformes (making apes a type of specialized monkey), or \\\"monkey\\\" has to be recognized purely as a grade term of convenience rather than a strict clade.\",[[0,23,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]],[[[null,[null,0,\"The Human Ego: Culturally, humans have spent millennia erecting philosophical and biological fences between \\\"us\\\" and \\\"animals\\\" (or specifically \\\"beasts\\\" and \\\"apes\\\"), making the realization that we are simply clever, hairless, bipedal simians hard to swallow for the easily offended.\",[[0,14,[[null,null,null,null,2]]]]]]]]]]],[null,[null,0,\"So, next time someone gets bent out of shape, remind them that refusing to be a simian is like trying to claim you're no longer a mammal just because you wear shoes.\"]],[null,[null,0,\"Does this strict phylogenetic view of \\\"monkey\\\" solve the semantic mess, or do you think common language will ever catch up to cladistics?\"]]]]],null,\"8c46e95b1a07cecc\",null,null,\"8c46e95b1a07cecc\",null,null,null,\"3.5 Flash-Lite\",null,null,6,1],[1789201699,86060000]]],null,null,[]]",null,null,null,"generic"]]
